We solve the heat equation in the half-space $z>0$ with a homogeneous Neumann condition at $z=0$ by the **method of images** (even reflection).

---

## Step 1. Even extension of the initial data

Define the even extension of $f$ across the plane $z=0$:

$$
\tilde f(x,y,z)=
\begin{cases}
f(x,y,z), & z\ge 0,\\[4pt]
f(x,y,-z), & z<0.
\end{cases}
$$

This is an even function of $z$ on all of $\mathbb R^3$, so $\tilde f$ is continuous across $z=0$ (provided $f$ is reasonable) and $\partial_z \tilde f\big|_{z=0^-}=-\partial_z f\big|_{z=0^+}$, i.e. $\partial_z\tilde f$ is odd in $z$.

## Step 2. Cauchy problem on the full space

Solve the Cauchy problem on $\mathbb R^3$:
$$
v_t=a^2\Delta v,\qquad v\big|_{t=0}=\tilde f.
$$
The Poisson formula gives
$$
v(x,y,z,t)=\frac{1}{(2a\sqrt{\pi t})^{3}}\iiint_{\mathbb R^3}\tilde f(\xi,\eta,\zeta)\,
\exp\!\Bigl(-\frac{(x-\xi)^2+(y-\eta)^2+(z-\zeta)^2}{4a^2 t}\Bigr)\,d\xi\,d\eta\,d\zeta .
$$

Since $\tilde f$ is even in $\zeta$ and the heat kernel is symmetric, $v$ is even in $z$; hence $v_z\big|_{z=0}=0$. Therefore the restriction $u:=v\big|_{z\ge 0}$ satisfies both the equation and the Neumann boundary condition, with $u\big|_{t=0}=f$.

## Step 3. Split the $\zeta$-integral

Write the $\zeta$-integral as $\int_{-\infty}^{0}+\int_{0}^{\infty}$. In the first piece substitute $\zeta'=-\zeta$ and use $\tilde f(\xi,\eta,\zeta)=f(\xi,\eta,-\zeta)=f(\xi,\eta,\zeta')$:

$$
\int_{-\infty}^{0}\tilde f(\xi,\eta,\zeta)\,e^{-\frac{(z-\zeta)^2}{4a^2t}}\,d\zeta
=\int_{0}^{\infty}f(\xi,\eta,\zeta')\,e^{-\frac{(z+\zeta')^2}{4a^2t}}\,d\zeta'.
$$

Combining both pieces (renaming $\zeta'\to\zeta$) yields the solution on the half-space:

$$
\boxed{
u(x,y,z,t)=\frac{1}{(2a\sqrt{\pi t})^{3}}\iint_{\mathbb R^2}\int_{0}^{\infty}
f(\xi,\eta,\zeta)\Bigl[
e^{-\frac{(x-\xi)^2+(y-\eta)^2+(z-\zeta)^2}{4a^2t}}
+
e^{-\frac{(x-\xi)^2+(y-\eta)^2+(z+\zeta)^2}{4a^2t}}
\Bigr]\,d\zeta\,d\xi\,d\eta .
}
$$

Equivalently, separating the $(x,y)$-convolution (Gaussian in the plane) from the $z$-part:

$$
u(x,y,z,t)=\frac{1}{4\pi a^2 t}\iint_{\mathbb R^2}e^{-\frac{(x-\xi)^2+(y-\eta)^2}{4a^2t}}
\Bigl[\frac{1}{2a\sqrt{\pi t}}\int_{0}^{\infty}f(\xi,\eta,\zeta)\Bigl(
e^{-\frac{(z-\zeta)^2}{4a^2t}}+e^{-\frac{(z+\zeta)^2}{4a^2t}}
\Bigr)d\zeta\Bigr]d\xi\,d\eta .
$$

## Step 4. Verification

**Equation.** Inside the domain $z>0$, $t>0$, the integrand is a sum of two full-space heat kernels acting on the bounded function $f$; each term satisfies $u_t=a^2\Delta u$, hence so does their sum.

**Initial condition.** As $t\to 0^+$, each Gaussian kernel tends to the Dirac delta. The first term concentrates at $(\xi,\eta,\zeta)=(x,y,z)$; the second term concentrates at $(\xi,\eta,\zeta)=(x,y,-z)$, which lies outside the integration domain $z>0$ (for $z>0$) and contributes nothing. Hence $u\to f(x,y,z)$.

**Neumann boundary condition.** Differentiate under the integral:
$$
u_z=\frac{1}{(2a\sqrt{\pi t})^3}\iint_{\mathbb R^2}\int_0^\infty f(\xi,\eta,\zeta)\Bigl[
-\frac{z-\zeta}{2a^2t}\,e^{-\frac{(z-\zeta)^2}{4a^2t}}
-\frac{z+\zeta}{2a^2t}\,e^{-\frac{(z+\zeta)^2}{4a^2t}}
\Bigr]d\zeta\,d\xi\,d\eta .
$$
Setting $z=0$:
$$
u_z\big|_{z=0}=\frac{1}{(2a\sqrt{\pi t})^3}\iint_{\mathbb R^2}\int_0^\infty f(\xi,\eta,\zeta)\Bigl[
\frac{\zeta}{2a^2t}\,e^{-\frac{\zeta^2}{4a^2t}}
-\frac{\zeta}{2a^2t}\,e^{-\frac{\zeta^2}{4a^2t}}
\Bigr]d\zeta\,d\xi\,d\eta =0,
$$
since the two terms cancel identically. Thus $u_z\big|_{z=0}=0$ as required.

---

The solution is the **even reflection (Neumann image) of the free-space heat kernel** applied to $f$:

$$
u(x,y,z,t)=\frac{1}{(2a\sqrt{\pi t})^{3}}\iint_{\mathbb R^2}\int_{0}^{\infty}
f(\xi,\eta,\zeta)\Bigl[
e^{-\frac{(x-\xi)^2+(y-\eta)^2+(z-\zeta)^2}{4a^2t}}
+
e^{-\frac{(x-\xi)^2+(y-\eta)^2+(z+\zeta)^2}{4a^2t}}
\Bigr]\,d\zeta\,d\xi\,d\eta .
$$

